#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100676 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100852 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4a852fb-e00b-55e8-bde5-d6f6e2278691" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of a Function Given a Graph Without Labeled Areas"}] :question/id #uuid "21f2e870-7d29-5175-bfb9-da53446c3a4c" :question/math-academy-id "e-430" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on the interval $[-2,3]$. The graph of $y=f'(x)$ is shown below. It consists of three line segments. Find $f(0)$ if ${f(2)=3}$.\n\n![](images/87/8712bb0e61664a6d2ce86b465de2ff5c2d87fd159aa120e5eb6cae633585fbb8.png)" :question/worked-solution "The fundamental theorem of calculus states that\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{2} f'(x) \\: \\textrm{d}x = f(2) - f(0)\n$$\n\nand we know that\n\n$$\nf(2)=3\n$$\n\nso we can find $f(0)$ if we can figure out the value of the integral.\n\nRemember that the integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis. To compute the area under the curve, let's split up the integral into two integrals which represent areas that are easier to compute:\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{2} f'(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x = \\underbrace{\\int_{0}^{1} f'(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x}_{A_1} + \\underbrace{\\int_{1}^{2} f'(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x}_{A_2}\n$$\n\nThe $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [0,1]$. The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{1} &= \\frac{1}{2}(a + b)h \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot (1 + 2) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [1,2]$. The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{2} &= \\frac{1}{2}(a + b)h \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot (1 + 2) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the value of the original integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{2}f^{′}(x)dx &= A_{1} + A_{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2} + \\frac{3}{2} \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the value above and the fact that\n\n$$\nf(2)=3\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3 &= f(2) - f(0) \\\\\n3 &= 3 - f(0) \\\\\nf(0) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100679 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100853 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64725151-b0e8-55f8-b910-358d4d2b1d36" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of a Function Given a Graph With Labeled Areas"}] :question/id #uuid "20da9010-55f7-5e44-ba73-1c881084e4b2" :question/math-academy-id "e-431" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on the interval $[-1,5]$. The graph of $y=f'(x)$ is shown below, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $1$, $2.5$, and $3.5$ respectively (from left to right). Find $f(-1)$ if ${f(5)=-2}$.\n\n![](images/a1/a135a999d2554c6deea4483b6bd6c67a2695d43c5b2ee90f5940d29918f0cf70.png)" :question/worked-solution "From the fundamental theorem of calculus, we find that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{-1}^{5}f^{′}(x)dx &= f(5) - f(-1) \\\\\n\\int_{-1}^{5}f^{′}(x)dx &= -2 - f(-1) \\\\\nf(-1) &= -2 - \\int_{-1}^{5}f^{′}(x)dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, remember that the integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where we take the area as negative if the curve lies below the $x$-axis. So, using the labels on the graph, the integral is\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^{5} f'(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x = 1 +(-2.5) + 3.5 = 2\n$$\n\nFinally, using the value of the integral, we can find $f(-1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(-1) &= -2 - \\int_{-1}^{5}f^{′}(x)dx \\\\\n&=-2 - 2 \\\\\n&=-4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101544 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100853 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64725151-b0e8-55f8-b910-358d4d2b1d36" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of a Function Given a Graph With Labeled Areas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "818d8244-7109-56c2-b289-6de6c0f3d992" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100426} {:answer/id #uuid "f85d2116-15cc-50a9-8559-10951c90f0b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100427} {:answer/id #uuid "fcba153f-dc67-53a4-bfae-164b3c4155db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100428} {:answer/id #uuid "0f579af9-7e72-5203-975b-be23f5467abe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100429} {:answer/id #uuid "3bf6a48b-5026-57b6-a427-7e0b4c2e9b15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100430}] :answer-field/id #uuid "da8fd295-caca-5d63-b39b-cf2ced3da971" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100425}] :question/id #uuid "f6efa7e3-8be8-56a3-a68d-9e8131a012bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-83002" :question/problem "![](images/88/881ec029bea9d85b0f1f76c3c62ca0343b9b6103c57122f6dc06d02cf5b30d8c.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on the interval $[- 4, 4]$. The graph of $y = f^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)$, is given above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $8, 5$ and $6$, respectively (from left to right). Find $f(4)$ if $f(-4) =-1$."}]}
